# Put a Staff of Winter in a character's hands, identify it, and open the
# item description screen, which prints the staff's page and the staff's
# weapon quality one under the other. Read by
# tools/check_staff_winter_quality.sh, finding PA-08-F6 of bd inc-tek.8.8.
#
# WHY THIS ORACLE AND NOT THE ITEM'S NAME. The entity carries EF_HIDEQUAL,
# and that flag suppresses the quality word in the item's NAME and nowhere
# else: src/Message.cpp:1241 guards the whole quality loop of Item::xName
# with it, so the staff is called "Staff +2 of Winter" and never "Staff +2 of
# numbing". No name-based check could ever see this defect. The description
# screen is a different code path -- QItem::Describe (src/Help.cpp:3508-3524)
# walks Qualities[] itself, with no EF_HIDEQUAL test -- so it prints the
# quality by name and then prints the quality's own paragraph from
# QualityDescs (src/Tables.cpp:1831-1832). That paragraph names the ability
# score the quality damages, which is the whole of this finding.
#
# WHY THE PACK IS IDENTIFIED. QItem::Describe skips any quality below
# IQ_DWARVEN that KnownQuality() denies (src/Help.cpp:3509), and Item::
# Describe prints the entity's page only when the item isKnown(KN_MAGIC)
# (src/Help.cpp:3405). Wizard mode's "Identify Whole Pack" calls
# MakeKnown(0xFF) on everything the character carries (src/Debug.cpp:717-720),
# and QItem::MakeKnown sets KnownQualities to 0xFF whenever KN_MAGIC arrives
# (inc/Item.h:235-237). Both halves of the contradiction are then on screen
# together.
#
# WHY A BARBARIAN IS ENOUGH. The staff's caster-level-three door is on its
# EV_MAGIC_HIT handler, which gates the EA_GRANT blocks. The weapon quality
# is not granted that way: ITEM_QUALITIES is applied when the item is made,
# so it is on the object itself and the description screen reads it off the
# object. tools/keys/chargen.keys makes an orc barbarian, and that is all
# this reading needs.
#
#   w y                    enter wizard mode and open its menu.
#   Item Acquisition       RIGHT*3 walks the glyph row to Staves: ItemGlyphs
#   RIGHT*3                (src/Tables.cpp:320-323) puts -AI_STAFF three
#                          steps along.
#   @until "Staff of       the candidate list has no menu letters, so the
#     the Abyss" DOWN      cursor is walked. Three rows show and the MIDDLE
#                          one is selected, so the run stops on the pass
#                          where the row BELOW the wanted one appears. The
#                          Abyss is the entity after Winter in
#                          lib/m_items.irh. The check does not trust that
#                          ordering: it reads the staff's name back off the
#                          description box's own title line.
#   ENTER 2 ENTER          take it, and answer "Enter Item Level:" with 2,
#                          which is the staff's own INITIAL_PLUS and the plus
#                          its page states (src/Debug.cpp:604-620).
#   b SPACE s              the pickup drops the staff into the in-air slot and
#                          opens Inventory Mode. 'b' is the Weapon Hand --
#                          the slot lettering skips 'd', which is the Drop
#                          key -- SPACE swaps the staff into it
#                          (src/Managers.cpp:873), and 's' stows the starting
#                          knife that swap pushed into the air, because the
#                          manager will not close while anything is held
#                          there (src/Managers.cpp:835-848). A quarterstaff
#                          is two-handed, so it fills the Ready Hand as well.
#   ESC i b x              leave the manager, reopen it once the pack is
#                          identified, put the cursor back on the Weapon Hand
#                          and press 'x', which describes what is in it
#                          (src/Managers.cpp:751-754).
#   DOWN*10                the description is longer than its box. The box
#                          scrolls one row per DOWN (src/TextTerm.cpp:537-540),
#                          and the quality paragraph is the last thing in it,
#                          because QItem::Describe appends the qualities
#                          after everything Item::Describe wrote.

@include chargen.keys

w
y
@choose "Item Acquisition"
RIGHT*3
@until "Staff of the Abyss" DOWN
ENTER
2
ENTER
b
SPACE
s
@dump:staff-wielded
ESC
w
@choose "Identify Whole Pack"
i
b
x
@dump:desc-top
DOWN*10
@dump:desc-bottom
ESC
ESC
